{:edb/db-before-t 2264 :edb/db-after-t 2265 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535577 50 #inst "2026-10-14T14:50:42.865Z" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518716 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518725 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518726 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518729 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518731 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518733 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518743 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518744 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518756 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186518758 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186152118 1040 #edb/ref 17592186518761 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518715 1080 "144(v+2)^{4}(w+6)^{9}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518715 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518715 1083 #uuid "163ce63a-d218-5bf5-a5ea-bd1ee7ff4767" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518747 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518716 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $7(u+4)^{2}(y-3)$ and $6(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518716 1072 #uuid "1d65105c-f360-50e1-a105-3d1c246de3be" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518716 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(7,6):$\n\n- Multiples of $7:$ $\\,7,\\,14,\\,21,\\,28,\\,35,\\,42,\\,49,…$\n\n- Multiples of $6:$ $\\,6,\\,12,\\,18,\\,24,\\,30,\\,36,\\,42,\\,48,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(7,6)=42.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(u+4)^{2}(y-3)$ and $(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $(u+4)$ and $(y-3)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(u+4)^{2}$ and $(u+4)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(y-3)$ and $(y-3)^{5},$ the largest power is $5.$\n\nNow, we raise $(u+4)$ and $(y-3)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((u+4)^{2}(y-3),\\,(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5})=(u+4)^{4}\\cdot (y-3)^{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(7(u+4)^{2}(y-3),\\,6(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5})=42(u+4)^{4}(y-3)^{5}.\n$$" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518717 1080 "80(v-3)^{8}(u-3)^{4}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518717 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518717 1083 #uuid "1dd84822-b64e-59e1-b2fa-d0ecdb8d68c6" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518718 1080 "(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{4}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518718 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518718 1083 #uuid "2448c69f-83d4-546e-ae12-74e766b73106" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518719 1080 "15(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{4}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518719 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518719 1083 #uuid "26feb717-e88e-57b9-aab0-455f57829c7b" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518720 1080 "80(v-3)^{6}(u-3)^{2}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518720 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518720 1083 #uuid "2cad1c6c-87d2-57ca-b200-b90fda50d1ac" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186518722 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518721 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518721 1077 "field-1" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518721 1078 #edb/ref 17592186518722 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518721 1079 #uuid "2fb19667-bc74-5697-9317-eef4ecd3463d" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518722 1080 "40(v+6)^{4}(y-5)^{3}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518722 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518722 1083 #uuid "303ed52d-40ae-51f6-ac2c-cd0a8e34ac56" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518723 1080 "144(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518723 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518723 1083 #uuid "337031ce-cfce-5412-b4ce-3bf42817e30c" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518724 1080 "140(u-5)^{7}(v+7)^{3}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518724 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518724 1083 #uuid "349edbbb-d0f8-596d-b975-2f8220606412" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518757 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518725 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $18(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}$ and $16(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518725 1072 #uuid "3b03d1f0-fd10-5108-8d61-995f78e9f43f" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518725 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(18,16):$\n\n- Multiples of $18:$ $\\,18,\\,36,\\,54,\\,72,\\,90,\\,108,\\,126,\\,144,\\,162,…$\n\n- Multiples of $16:$ $\\,16,\\,32,\\,48,\\,64,\\,80,\\,96,\\,112,\\,128,\\,144,\\,160,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(18,16)=144.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}$ and $(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8},$ we find the largest powers for $(w+6)$ and $(z-4)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(w+6)^{4}$ and $(w+6)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(z-4)^{9}$ and $(z-4)^{8},$ the largest power is $9.$\n\nNow, we raise $(w+6)$ and $(z-4)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9},\\,(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8})=(w+6)^{4}\\cdot (z-4)^{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(18(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9},\\,16(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{8})=144(w+6)^{4}(z-4)^{9}.\n$$" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518760 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518726 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518726 1071 "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $21{z}^{3}(w-1)^{3}$ and $24{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8}$." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518726 1072 #uuid "3bbfe5df-ca8e-542a-98e0-48714818b738" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518726 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(21,24):$\n\n- Multiples of $21:$ $\\,21,\\,42,\\,63,\\,84,\\,105,\\,126,\\,147,\\,168,\\,189,…$\n\n- Multiples of $24:$ $\\,24,\\,48,\\,72,\\,96,\\,120,\\,144,\\,168,\\,192,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(21,24)=168.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of ${z}^{3}(w-1)^{3}$ and ${z}^{6}(w-1)^{8},$ we find the largest powers for $z$ and $(w-1)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For ${z}^{3}$ and ${z}^{6},$ the largest power is $6.$\n\n- For $(w-1)^{3}$ and $(w-1)^{8},$ the largest power is $8.$\n\nNow, we raise $z$ and $(w-1)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({z}^{3}(w-1)^{3},\\,{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8})={z}^{6}\\cdot (w-1)^{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(21{z}^{3}(w-1)^{3},\\,24{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8})=168{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8}.\n$$" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518727 1080 "140(u-5)^{14}(v+7)^{12}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518727 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518727 1083 #uuid "464ff73f-6d3f-5219-8053-3f5d882e2d3c" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518728 1080 "168{z}^{8}(w-1)^{6}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518728 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518728 1083 #uuid "4bdb3e46-73c5-57dc-9f72-d9f33bccbd67" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518729 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518738 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518729 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518729 1071 "Find the least common multiple of the polynomials $5(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{3}$ and $3(u-7)^{2}(z+4)^{4}$." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518729 1072 #uuid "4e34e2b8-4159-5cf7-a7db-15ca437cba97" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518729 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(5,3):$\n\n- Multiples of $5:$ $\\,5,\\,10,\\,15,\\,20,…$\n\n- Multiples of $3:$ $\\,3,\\,6,\\,9,\\,12,\\,15,\\,18,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(5,3)=15.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{3}$ and $(u-7)^{2}(z+4)^{4},$ we find the largest powers for $(u-7)$ and $(z+4)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(u-7)^{3}$ and $(u-7)^{2},$ the largest power is $3.$\n\n- For $(z+4)^{3}$ and $(z+4)^{4},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\nNow, we raise $(u-7)$ and $(z+4)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{3},\\,(u-7)^{2}(z+4)^{4})=(u-7)^{3}\\cdot (z+4)^{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(5(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{3},\\,3(u-7)^{2}(z+4)^{4})=15(u-7)^{3}(z+4)^{4}.\n$$" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518730 1080 "168{z}^{6}(w-1)^{8}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518730 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518730 1083 #uuid "50e88fcd-399b-55b2-a76d-293fbd039f32" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518731 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518740 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518731 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518731 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $14(u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{2}$ and $22(u-1)^{5}(x+6)^{7}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518731 1072 #uuid "50fd899f-3db1-5330-b39c-e7477a9b6989" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518731 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(14,22):$\n\n- Multiples of $14:$ $\\,14,\\,28,\\,42,\\,56,\\,70,\\,84,\\,98,\\,112,\\,126,\\,140,\\,154,\\,168,…$\n\n- Multiples of $22:$ $\\,22,\\,44,\\,66,\\,88,\\,110,\\,132,\\,154,\\,176,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(14,22)=154.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{2}$ and $(u-1)^{5}(x+6)^{7},$ we find the largest powers for $(u-1)$ and $(x+6)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(u-1)^{9}$ and $(u-1)^{5},$ the largest power is $9.$\n\n- For $(x+6)^{2}$ and $(x+6)^{7},$ the largest power is $7.$\n\nNow, we raise $(u-1)$ and $(x+6)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{2},\\,(u-1)^{5}(x+6)^{7})=(u-1)^{9}\\cdot (x+6)^{7}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results together:\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(14(u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{2},\\,22(u-1)^{5}(x+6)^{7})=154(u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{7}.\n$$" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518732 1080 "154(u-1)^{9}(x+6)^{7}" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518732 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518732 1083 #uuid "58241d84-45de-5375-97d7-b11371ff1bdb" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518733 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518742 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518733 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518733 1071 "The least common multiple of the polynomials $16(v+2)^{4}(w+6)^{9}$ and $18(v+2)^{2}(w+6)^{5}$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518733 1072 #uuid "586aaec0-24f5-5011-9821-30d3166fbbd9" 13194139535577 17592186045443 true] [17592186518733 1074 "To find the least common multiple ($\\text{LCM}$) of two polynomials that are made up of products of binomials, we find the least common multiple of the constants and the least common multiple of each variable part. Then, we multiply all of the results together.\n\nSo first, we find $\\text{LCM}(16,18):$\n\n- Multiples of $16:$ $\\,16,\\,32,\\,48,\\,64,\\,80,\\,96,\\,112,\\,128,\\,144,\\,160,…$\n\n- Multiples of $18:$ $\\,18,\\,36,\\,54,\\,72,\\,90,\\,108,\\,126,\\,144,\\,162,…$\n\nSo, $\\text{LCM}(16,18)=144.$\n\nTo find the $\\text{LCM}$ of $(v+2)^{4}(w+6)^{9}$ and $(v+2)^{2}(w+6)^{5},$ we find the largest powers for $(v+2)$ and $(w+6)$ that appear in the expressions:\n\n- For $(v+2)^{4}$ and $(v+2)^{2},$ the largest power is $4.$\n\n- For $(w+6)^{9}$ and $(w+6)^{5},$ the largest power is $9.$\n\nNow, we raise $(v+2)$ and $(w+6)$ to the corresponding largest powers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}((v+2)^{4}(w+6)^{9},\\,(v+2)^{2}(w+6)^{5})=(v+2)^{4}\\cdot (w+6)^{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we multiply all of our results tog